"""Original LLD and API teaching, continuing the payment/notification examples."""
from teaching.foundations import put, LESSONS
put(5, '''A payment class that validates an order, creates a provider SDK, calculates fees, and writes audit records hides several reasons to change. Abstraction exposes charge(command); encapsulation protects payment state; polymorphism permits different implementations of a behavior. Inheritance is one mechanism for sharing and specializing behavior, not a requirement for these benefits. Extract provider communication behind a contract while keeping the amount and allowed state transitions under application control. || PaymentService handles intent / Provider contract hides SDK / Payment state protects transitions / Audit records observable outcomes

A subclass can violate assumptions inherited by callers: a ReadOnlyAccount subclass that throws from withdraw is not substitutable for a contract promising withdrawal. Composition combines collaborators through explicit contracts instead. A payment service receiving a fraud policy and a gateway can vary them independently without a subclass for every combination. Composition introduces wiring and interface design; inheritance remains useful when the behavioral relationship is genuinely stable, rather than merely when two classes share fields. || Subclass combines unrelated changes / Policy and gateway vary independently / Compose collaborators at startup / Test behavior at each contract

A gateway interface must specify currency, amount units, timeouts, duplicate identity, and error meaning. charge(100) is ambiguous: one currency unit, one hundred, or one hundred minor units? Return a structured outcome such as approved, declined, or uncertain with a provider reference. A transport exception after submission is not proof of decline. Behavioral contracts let adapters be replaced without silently changing business semantics, but require integration tests against real provider behavior. || Command: 100 minor units in USD / Stable operation identity supplied / Outcome: approved, declined or uncertain / Transport failure is not business decline

Cohesion improves when related payment rules live together; coupling falls when business code does not depend on provider-specific field names. Measure the effect using a change: adding an SMS receipt should not alter ledger arithmetic. Several focused modules may still share one deployment. Excessive fragmentation can make a simple path harder to follow, so extract a responsibility when it owns an invariant, hides unstable knowledge, or supports a concrete change independently. || Fee policy changes / Provider schema stays unchanged / Receipt adapter changes / Ledger invariant stays unchanged

SOLID is a set of design heuristics: single responsibility, open/closed extension, behavioral substitution, focused interfaces, and dependency inversion. A payment service can depend on a narrow charge contract while its adapter depends on an SDK. These ideas do not require one interface per class or forbid editing existing code. An extension mechanism with twenty configuration switches may be harder to understand than a deliberate edit. Judge each heuristic against the actual change and testing cost. || Separate reasons to change / Preserve behavioral substitution / Keep collaborator contracts narrow / Invert unstable dependency knowledge

DRY avoids duplicated knowledge, not every repeated line. KISS prefers a design understandable for the required behavior; YAGNI defers speculative capability. Two similar validations may represent different policies and should not be merged solely for visual similarity. Conversely, copying the same currency conversion rule into every gateway creates a real consistency risk. Share a stable business concept after identifying its owner, and keep a reversible simple implementation until evidence justifies a larger abstraction. || Similar syntax may differ in meaning / Repeated business rule has one owner / Extract only stable shared knowledge / Avoid speculative configuration layers

The Law of Demeter discourages reaching deeply through another object's internal graph. order.customer.account.settings.currency exposes representation and creates change propagation. Ask an appropriate owner for the information or behavior needed, such as order.paymentCurrency(). It is a heuristic, not a ban on every fluent API or data traversal. Adding dozens of meaningless forwarding methods can obscure the model; the objective is to reduce knowledge of unstable structure, not count dots in source code. || Caller traverses internal object graph / Representation changes in one owner / Many callers break / Expose a purposeful operation instead

Tell, Don't Ask encourages sending an intention to the state owner. Rather than reading account.balance, comparing it outside, and writing it back, request reserve(amount) so the account can enforce its invariant. Reading a value for a report remains appropriate. The principle does not authorize hiding surprising side effects behind getters. The owner must still coordinate concurrent changes at the true shared-state boundary, such as an atomic database transaction. || Caller requests reserve(500) / Owner checks available funds / Owner updates reserved balance / Caller receives explicit outcome

Command-query separation distinguishes operations that change state from queries that report it. A payment status getter should not unexpectedly charge a card. Some practical commands return useful results, such as the created payment identifier, without violating the underlying intent of clear side effects. Document these conventions. Separating read and write models into CQRS is a larger architectural choice; ordinary command-query separation does not require separate services, databases, or event sourcing. || Query reads payment status / Command requests a payment / Command may return operation ID / CQRS is a separate design choice

Immutable values make state transitions easier to inspect: compute a new PaymentSummary from an old value rather than mutating a shared object in several helpers. Keep effectful operations at explicit boundaries and pass their results into pure calculations. Immutability can cost allocation and copying, and cannot undo a provider charge. An immutable command still needs idempotency and authorization when executed; local referential transparency is not an end-to-end transaction guarantee. || Pure fee calculation / New immutable payment value / Explicit gateway side effect / Persist outcome under a transaction

An entity has identity across changes; a value object is defined by its value; a domain model expresses meaningful rules. Payment 42 remains the same entity after status changes. Money(USD,100) is a value that should reject incompatible arithmetic or apply an explicit conversion policy. A domain service can coordinate a rule that belongs to no single entity. Avoid an anemic model that scatters every invariant across controllers, while keeping trivial data transport simple. || Payment entity: ID 42 / Money value: USD 100 minor units / Transition preserves amount invariant / Domain service coordinates a business rule

An object collaboration describes messages and ownership, not just fields. The checkout orchestrator asks inventory to reserve, asks payments to authorize, and records order state. Neither inventory nor payments should silently mutate the other's private records. Make results explicit so partial failure can be handled. Too much conversational object traffic can become a distributed performance problem if moved across network boundaries; a good in-process collaboration is not automatically a good remote API. || Checkout requests reservation / Inventory returns reservation ID / Checkout requests authorization / Order records explicit outcomes

Testability follows controllable inputs, visible outcomes, and explicit effects. Inject a fake gateway to test declined-payment behavior without network calls, then test the real adapter against a contract environment. A mock expectation that asserts every internal method call can make refactoring expensive while proving little business behavior. Prefer invariant and outcome tests, and retain integration coverage for transactions, serialization, timeouts, and resource cleanup that a fake cannot faithfully establish. || Fake returns decline / Service records declined outcome / No shipment is initiated / Real adapter has separate contract checks

Refactoring preserves intended behavior while improving structure. Start by characterizing the tangled payment class: valid charge, decline, provider timeout, and persistence failure. Then extract parsing, fee calculation, gateway calls, and storage incrementally. A long method, duplicated conditional, or feature envy is evidence to investigate, not proof that a design pattern is required. Keep tests focused on observable obligations so moving code does not change payment semantics accidentally. || Characterize current outcomes / Extract one responsibility / Re-run behavioral checks / Compare failures before and after

A functional design can represent payment decisions as pure transformations of values, with effects interpreted at the boundary. decide(state, command) may yield events and requested external actions; an interpreter commits events or calls a gateway. This reduces hidden mutation and makes replaying logic easier. It still needs ownership, durable execution, and effect deduplication. Objects and functions are complementary tools; choose the representation that makes invariants and collaboration clearest in the language and team context. || State plus command / Pure decision produces events / Effect interpreter executes actions / Durable owner reconciles outcomes''')

put(6, '''A notification service that imports a concrete email SDK knows both business behavior and infrastructure details. Dependency direction describes that source-level knowledge; inversion makes high-level policy depend on an abstract sending contract that adapters implement. Runtime calls still reach a concrete implementation. Merely moving the SDK constructor into a helper does not invert the dependency if the service still consumes provider-specific types and exceptions. Protect the semantic contract as well as the import graph. || Notification policy / Depends on Sender contract / Email adapter implements contract / Runtime call reaches concrete adapter

Inversion of Control means some control is delegated to a framework or external coordinator: an event loop invokes your callback, or a host constructs your request handler. Dependency Injection is one way to supply collaborators under that arrangement, not a synonym for every form of IoC. Registration order, callback lifetime, and failure propagation still need documentation. Giving control to a framework relocates decisions; it does not remove responsibility for them. || Application registers callback / Framework owns dispatch / Framework invokes handler / Handler owns business behavior

Dependency Injection supplies a collaborator rather than constructing or locating it internally. NotificationService(sender) can run with email, SMS, or a recording test sender. The composition root chooses the concrete implementation. DI can be ordinary argument passing; a container is optional. If the caller still supplies one giant object exposing every infrastructure dependency, the design retains broad coupling despite using injection syntax. Keep the contract narrow and its behavior explicit. || Construct EmailSender / Pass sender into service / Service invokes send(message) / Test substitutes RecordingSender

Constructor injection makes mandatory dependencies available throughout an object's valid lifetime. Setter injection can model optional or later configuration but risks partially initialized objects. Method injection supplies a collaborator only for one operation and makes per-call variation explicit. Do not store a request-specific dependency passed to a long-lived object unless ownership and concurrency permit it. Select the injection form from required lifetime and validity, rather than assuming framework conventions are universal. || Constructor: mandatory sender / Setter: optional observer / Method: one-call formatter / Lifetime must match actual ownership

Dependency inversion is a design principle about which knowledge depends on which abstraction. DI is a construction technique. A service can receive a concrete SDK through its constructor and still violate the intended abstraction boundary; conversely, a manually wired function can depend on a stable protocol without using a container. Review provider-specific errors, schemas, and resource handles crossing the boundary. Syntax alone cannot prove that high-level policy has been isolated from volatile details. || Concrete SDK injected / Source still depends on SDK semantics / Introduce application-owned protocol / Injection and inversion now cooperate

The composition root builds the application graph in one comprehensible place. A DI container automates construction and lifetime resolution; it can detect some invalid graphs but can also hide dependencies and defer errors until runtime. Validate registrations at startup and prefer explicit ownership for disposable resources. For three collaborators, manual construction may be easiest. Use container features when they solve real wiring or integration complexity, not to turn every value into a globally resolvable service. || Startup selects implementations / Container or code constructs graph / Validate graph and ownership / Application uses resolved collaborators

Transient means a resolution creates a new instance; scoped means instances are reused within a defined scope; singleton means reuse within the container or application boundary specified by the framework. None implies global uniqueness across processes. If two request scopes each resolve a scoped unit of work twice, they see identities A,A and B,B. A singleton shared by many requests must meet its concurrency contract. Framework names and disposal rules differ and must be checked. || Scope 1: scoped A then A / Scope 2: scoped B then B / Transient: C then D / Singleton: S in both scopes

A request scope lasts for one request, while a session scope can span many requests and hold different privacy or memory risks. Custom job scopes are useful for background processing independent of HTTP. Do not let a job borrow a request-scoped connection after the response has disposed it. Create a job scope with a defined end and propagate only necessary immutable identifiers. A scope is an ownership convention, not automatically a database transaction or authenticated-user boundary. || HTTP request opens scope R / Response ends and disposes R / Background job opens its own J / Job closes J after work finishes

Creation, reference, and ownership are different relationships. A factory may create a connection while the caller owns disposal; a container may create and retain it until scope end. Two components holding a reference must not both assume exclusive closing rights. Write ownership into the contract. Returning a lazily evaluated query from a disposed repository is a common lifetime bug even when the return type matches; materialize bounded data or transfer an explicit streaming owner. || Factory creates resource / Owner retains disposal responsibility / Borrower uses within lifetime / Owner closes exactly at boundary

Cleanup must run on success, failure, and cancellation. Use structured scopes or finally blocks to return pool permits, close streams, and roll back unfinished transactions. Garbage collection is not a timely release guarantee for external resources. Cleanup can itself fail, so preserve the primary failure while recording cleanup problems. Resource lifetimes affect capacity: leaking one database connection per failed request can exhaust a pool even if the healthy request path is fast. || Acquire connection / Execute operation / Error or cancellation occurs / Finally returns or closes resource

Lazy initialization defers creation until the resource is needed. This can avoid unused work, but moves latency and failure to the first caller and introduces a concurrent initialization race. Define whether failed initialization is retried, cached, or surfaced permanently. A lock or runtime lazy primitive must protect publication as required. Warming essential dependencies at startup may provide clearer readiness behavior when accepting traffic without them would immediately fail. || First caller requests resource / Initialize once under coordination / Publish fully initialized value / Define retry after initialization failure

A dependency cycle A→B→A often signals mixed responsibilities or missing coordination; breaking it with a service locator can hide rather than solve the model. A captive dependency occurs when a long-lived owner retains a shorter-lived collaborator, such as a singleton keeping a request's unit of work. Refactor the operation to receive a valid scope or narrow factory. The fix must preserve ownership and authorization context rather than merely satisfy the container's graph validator. || Singleton captures request object / Request ends and disposes object / Later caller uses invalid dependency / Pass a scope-safe operation instead

DI makes collaborators visible in construction or function parameters. A service locator lets code request them from a registry, hiding dependencies behind runtime lookups. Locators can be appropriate at a deliberate integration boundary, but application code that reaches into a global locator is harder to reason about and test. Avoid replacing constructor arguments with one injected container; that often preserves the locator pattern under a different name. Resolve dependencies near the composition boundary. || Explicit constructor lists dependencies / Locator defers discovery to runtime / Missing registration fails later / Resolve near composition boundary

A factory encapsulates creation or selection; DI supplies an already selected collaborator or a factory itself. A notification service receiving sender_factory can choose a channel at runtime without importing concrete SDKs. The factory's returned lifetime must be clear: is each sender owned by the caller, shared, or scoped? Do not dispose a shared singleton as though every factory call created a private object. These patterns answer different questions and frequently work together. || Inject channel factory / Select SMS for this command / Factory returns defined-lifetime sender / Caller honors ownership contract

Creational patterns organize construction. Factory Method varies creation through a creator operation; Abstract Factory supplies a compatible family; Builder assembles a complex value through explicit steps; Prototype copies a configured instance; Singleton constrains instance access within a specified boundary. They are alternatives only when solving the same construction problem. A simple constructor is better when it already expresses all valid states. Pay particular attention to invalid partial builders, shallow-copy aliases, and global mutable singleton state. || Need valid object construction / Choose variation or assembly mechanism / Establish ownership and invariants / Avoid unnecessary global state

Structural patterns organize collaboration: Adapter translates an interface; Bridge separates two independently varying dimensions; Composite treats leaves and groups uniformly; Decorator adds behavior around a collaborator; Facade offers a focused entry point; Flyweight shares immutable intrinsic state; Proxy controls access to a target. They do not all mean “wrapper.” Compare the intent and preserved contract. A decorator that retries a non-idempotent charge changes observable semantics and needs more justification than a transparent logging wrapper. || Existing collaborator contract / Translate, compose, share or guard / Preserve stated behavior / Account for added calls and state

Behavioral patterns organize decisions and coordination. Strategy selects interchangeable algorithms; Observer notifies subscribers; Command represents an intention; State varies behavior by lifecycle; Chain routes a request through handlers; Template Method fixes a skeleton with extension points; Iterator traverses; Mediator coordinates; Memento captures recoverable local state; Visitor adds operations over a known structure. Each introduces a particular extension boundary. Choose it when that boundary matches expected change; do not add a pattern merely to name a few conditionals. || Behavior varies / Identify the axis of variation / Choose an explicit coordination contract / Compare with direct functions and conditionals

Repository mediates domain-oriented access to persistent data; Unit of Work groups changes under one commit decision; Specification represents a reusable predicate; CQRS separates command and query models where their needs justify it. A repository should not pretend remote calls and local transactions share identical failure semantics. A Unit of Work must own its transaction and cleanup. CQRS can be implemented inside one process and one database; event sourcing and independent services are optional additional choices. || Repository exposes domain access / Unit of Work owns commit / Specification expresses predicate / CQRS separates model responsibilities''')

put(7, '''Layered architecture separates presentation, application behavior, domain policy, and infrastructure at a chosen granularity. Dependencies should be intentional: a controller should not bypass a domain invariant merely because an SQL helper is nearby. Layers can run in one process; physical N-tier deployment is a separate decision. Avoid mandatory pass-through classes that add no policy or abstraction. Trace one change, such as a new pricing rule, to verify that the layer boundary actually localizes knowledge. || Controller parses input / Application coordinates use case / Domain enforces rule / Infrastructure implements persistence

A modular monolith is deployed as a unit while its modules maintain explicit boundaries and ownership. Orders, catalog, and payments can have separate interfaces and schema ownership in one application. This keeps local calls and coordinated deployment simple while retaining future extraction options. Shared tables writable by every module defeat much of the benefit. Enforce module dependencies in code review or automated checks, and make cross-module operations pass through agreed contracts. || One deployment / Catalog owns product representation / Orders owns purchase lifecycle / Payments owns payment outcomes

Microservices add independently deployable processes and data ownership boundaries, along with network failures, version skew, and distributed operations. A monolith can be modular; a set of services can be tightly coupled. Extract inventory when independent scaling, failure isolation, or team ownership gives a concrete benefit. If every change requires simultaneous deployment of five services, independence is mostly nominal. Compare operational cost and transaction complexity against the change that motivated extraction. || Modular application / Identify independent operating need / Extract one stable boundary / Accept network and consistency costs

Clean Architecture places business policy inside dependencies on application-owned abstractions; framework and storage details depend inward. The drawing's circles describe source dependency rules, not the physical path of runtime messages. A use case might call an injected repository implemented by an outer adapter. Avoid leaking ORM entities or HTTP response types into core policy. The cost is extra mapping and contracts; a small CRUD tool may need only a modest version of the same separation. || Domain policy at center / Use case depends on port / Adapter implements port / Runtime call crosses outward through contract

Hexagonal architecture names inbound and outbound ports with adapters around the application. A CLI and HTTP controller can invoke the same create-order port; an SQL repository and test fake implement a storage port. Ports express the application's needs rather than every operation a vendor SDK offers. Network isolation is not implied by the hexagon. Test the core with controlled adapters and separately verify serialization, transaction behavior, and failures at real boundaries. || HTTP adapter to inbound port / CLI adapter to same port / Use case invokes outbound port / SQL adapter implements persistence

Onion Architecture also emphasizes dependencies toward domain policy, often organizing domain, application services, and infrastructure as concentric responsibilities. Names and layer placement vary across communities. Do not argue that one diagram guarantees better design than another with equivalent obligations. Ask whether the order rules can run independently of the web framework and whether storage errors remain explicit. Architectural styles are organizing tools; the concrete contracts and change behavior determine their usefulness. || Domain rule / Application coordination / Infrastructure implementation / Dependencies preserve policy independence

Domain-Driven Design builds a shared model of a complex business with domain experts. Its value is making ambiguous concepts and invariants explicit, not turning every database row into a class. In shopping, “available inventory” may differ between merchandising and reservation processing. Explore those meanings before choosing service boundaries. For a simple reference table, elaborate aggregates and events may cost more than they clarify; apply modeling effort where business behavior is genuinely complex. || Talk through a business scenario / Identify ambiguous terms / Name rules and invariants / Model the behavior together

A bounded context defines where a particular model and vocabulary are valid. The catalog's Product can emphasize description and merchandising; fulfillment's Item can emphasize a physical unit and location. Translating between them is deliberate, not evidence that one universal Product schema is missing. Ubiquitous language means consistent domain language inside a context, not forcing every team to use one model. Map interactions and ownership before deciding whether a context is a separate process. || Catalog Product model / Translation boundary / Fulfillment Item model / Each context owns its meaning

An aggregate is a consistency boundary whose root controls changes to its members. An Order might protect the relationship between line items and total amount within a transaction. Entities carry identity; value objects represent interchangeable values; domain services express rules spanning concepts. A huge aggregate containing the entire shop creates contention and awkward loading. Keep invariants that must change atomically together, and handle cross-aggregate coordination explicitly rather than assuming object references imply distributed transactions. || Command reaches aggregate root / Root validates local invariant / Members change atomically / Cross-aggregate work needs a protocol

A domain event records a meaningful fact such as OrderPlaced, not a vague instruction to “do something.” Include stable identity, time semantics, and enough versioned information for intended consumers without exposing every internal field. Recording an event in memory is not reliable publication. If state and publication intent must agree, persist them atomically through an outbox or another justified mechanism. Consumers must still handle duplicates, delayed delivery, and compatible schema evolution. || Order transition commits / OrderPlaced intent persisted / Relay publishes event / Consumers deduplicate and interpret version

Dependency rules constrain which modules can know about others. A payments module should expose a payment contract rather than allow orders to update its tables directly. Enforce these rules with import boundaries, schema permissions, and architecture tests where useful. Cycles often reveal unclear coordination. A shared utility can become a disguised common domain dependency; prefer explicit data translations when two contexts deliberately have different meanings for apparently similar concepts. || Orders uses payment contract / Payments owns payment tables / Direct table write is rejected / Boundary checked in source and storage

Business logic determines allowed outcomes, orchestration sequences collaborators, and infrastructure performs technical effects. “A refunded order cannot ship” is business logic; coordinating a refund request and status update is orchestration; calling the provider is infrastructure. One function may initially perform all three, but naming the distinctions reveals where tests and failure handling belong. Do not move a business rule into a message broker configuration merely because orchestration happens to be asynchronous. || Policy decides allowed transition / Orchestrator requests effects / Adapter communicates externally / Result returns to explicit state machine

Controllers translate transport, services coordinate application behavior, repositories mediate data, and adapters implement boundary protocols. These roles are conventions, not mandatory suffixes on class names. An application service can be a function. A domain service has a different purpose from a network-deployed service. Review behavior rather than the directory tree: if a controller owns transaction retries and pricing rules, creating a services folder has not actually separated responsibilities. || Transport data to command / Application use case / Domain-oriented data access / Boundary-specific implementation

An anti-corruption layer translates a foreign model into the local model so external terminology and quirks do not spread. A legacy provider may call a pending authorization “settled”; the payment context must translate that carefully instead of accepting the label as its own settlement state. The layer adds mappings and reconciliation work. Preserve unmapped states explicitly and fail safely rather than silently coercing every unknown value into success. || Legacy provider state / Explicit semantic translation / Local payment state / Unknown state remains an actionable error

Event-driven architecture connects components through events with independent processing. It reduces synchronous coupling in some paths while creating temporal coupling, replay, ordering, and observability obligations. An order can become accepted before a receipt is delivered, but inventory and payment requirements still determine which steps can be deferred. A broker does not supply end-to-end business reliability automatically. Make event ownership, deduplication, persistence, and reconciliation part of the architecture, not an afterthought. || Durable order event / Broker stores or routes message / Consumer updates its state / Reconciliation detects incomplete outcomes

Serverless functions can provide managed invocation and elastic execution, but do not remove systems design. Time limits, cold starts, concurrency caps, retries, identity, and durable state all affect behavior. Keep function-local memory as an optimization unless the platform explicitly guarantees more. A function processing a payment event must deduplicate effects across retries. Function-oriented organization can fit small independently triggered tasks; long workflows usually need an explicit durable state owner or workflow engine. || Event invokes function / Function receives bounded execution / External state persists outcome / Retry may invoke another instance

Evolutionary architecture changes in response to evidence while protecting important properties. Add fitness checks for module boundaries, migration compatibility, latency budgets, or tenant isolation so frequent changes do not silently erode them. A check must express a useful property rather than freeze incidental implementation. An ADR records why a boundary exists and what observation should trigger reconsideration. Evolution is not constant rewriting; many successful changes remove complexity rather than introduce another platform. || Change is proposed / Fitness checks test required properties / Evidence supports a reversible step / Decision rationale evolves with workload

Technical boundaries group by mechanism, such as controllers or databases; business boundaries group coherent capabilities, such as orders or fulfillment. Both can coexist at different levels. Splitting a company into an “SQL service” and “validation service” often makes every business change cross the network. Start with ownership of decisions and data, then choose internal technical layers. A bounded context is a modeling boundary; it is not automatically equivalent to a team, repository, or deployed service. || Business capability owns decisions / Internal layers implement mechanics / External contract hides representation / Deployment follows an actual need

A distributed monolith has several processes but little independent change or operation. Symptoms include synchronous chains for every action, shared writable schemas, coordinated releases, and failures cascading across services. Moving calls onto a network without clarifying ownership makes debugging harder and adds partial failure. Consolidating unstable boundaries into a modular deployment can be an improvement. Alternatively, redesign contracts, data ownership, and asynchronous coordination before claiming independent services have been achieved. || Several services share schema / Every release deploys all services / One dependency stalls the chain / Clarify ownership or consolidate boundary

Avoid microservices when the domain is still changing rapidly, the team cannot operate independent services safely, or scale and isolation do not require them. A modular monolith with measured bottlenecks and tested backups can be a stronger starting point. This is not a claim that microservices are inherently wrong: stable capabilities with distinct traffic and ownership can justify them. Evaluate the complete cost of transactions, deployment, telemetry, security, and incident response before extraction. || Small team and changing model / Modular deployment satisfies workload / Measure concrete scaling or ownership pain / Extract only a justified capability''')

put(8, '''An API is a communication contract, including inputs, outputs, side effects, error semantics, identity, deadlines, and compatibility. For create-payment, specify integer minor units and currency, distinguish a declined payment from an uncertain transport outcome, and identify who may access its status. A schema alone cannot describe whether a repeated request charges twice. Write example success, validation failure, duplicate, and lost-response histories before selecting a transport framework. || Client sends payment intent / Server validates identity and shape / Operation has explicit side effect / Result defines retry and uncertainty

REST organizes resource-oriented interactions using HTTP semantics; GraphQL exposes a typed query graph; gRPC commonly offers schema-defined RPC with streaming; RPC generally models operations. None is inherently correct for every boundary. GraphQL can reduce over-fetching but needs query-cost control and field authorization. RPC can make domain actions clear but still needs compatibility and retry semantics. Compare client needs, caching, tooling, latency, and evolution using the same operation, not only request syntax. || Same payment business operation / Resource API or operation API / Schema and authorization obligations / Transport does not decide correctness

Resources should reflect stable business identities. POST /payments creates an operation; GET /payments/{id} reports its state; POST /payments/{id}/refunds creates a separately tracked refund. Hiding every action under one generic /execute endpoint loses useful semantics, but forcing every domain action into CRUD can also obscure behavior. A refund resource needs its own amount, authorization, idempotency identity, and lifecycle. Use ownership and state transitions to guide the model. || Payment resource ID / Independent refund resource ID / Refund references original payment / Both have explicit status transitions

Validate structural shape and business meaning separately. A syntactically valid amount of −100 may violate the payment operation; an unknown field can be ignored or rejected according to compatibility policy. Bound payload size, collection lengths, and accepted encodings before expensive processing. Return machine-readable error codes with safe messages and a request identifier. Do not echo secrets or provider internals in validation errors. Schema evolution must consider clients that deserialize strictly as well as those that ignore extensions. || Bound request bytes / Parse declared schema / Validate business invariant / Return stable safe error model

HTTP status codes describe the protocol outcome, while an operation body may describe asynchronous business state. 201 can identify a created payment resource that is still pending; 202 means accepted for processing, not financially approved. A timeout has no definitive business meaning. Use stable application codes for validation, conflict, authorization, and retry advice. Do not translate every provider error into a generic 500 if clients need to distinguish a correctable request from an uncertain submitted operation. || HTTP request accepted / Payment resource is pending / Later business outcome arrives / Client uses status endpoint to reconcile

Pagination bounds work and response size. Define stable sorting, tie-breakers, allowed filters, maximum page size, and authorization at the query boundary. Returning “all payments” can exhaust memory and expose data even when the endpoint looks simple. Sorting by created_at alone is ambiguous for equal timestamps; append a unique identifier. Offset and cursor approaches differ under concurrent insertion, and neither automatically guarantees a consistent snapshot of a changing collection. || Filter authorized records / Sort by time then unique ID / Apply bounded page size / Return continuation metadata

Offset pagination skips a number of rows and is easy to express, but inserts before the offset can create duplicates or omissions between pages. Keyset cursors encode the last ordered key, such as (created_at,id), to continue after that position. They avoid some skip cost and shift anomalies but still need a consistency contract for changing rows. Sign or validate opaque cursors and bind them to the applicable filters and tenant context. || Page ends at key (100,42) / New row inserted before that key / Continue after (100,42) / Cursor remains bound to query and tenant

Compatible evolution preserves supported client behavior. Adding an optional field may be safe for tolerant clients but break strict validators or exhaustive enum handling. Changing an amount from minor to major units is breaking even if its type remains numeric. Use versioned semantics, overlap windows, telemetry on old consumers, and contract tests. A new URL version is only one technique; it does not remove the obligation to migrate clients and preserve stored event interpretation. || Existing client contract / Proposed semantic change / Compatibility test and migration window / Retire old behavior with evidence

An idempotency key identifies one logical operation across attempts. Store its owner, request fingerprint, execution state, and committed result under a uniqueness and atomicity rule. The same key with a different payload must conflict. If the provider may have charged before a timeout, use its stable operation key or reconcile before resubmitting. Retention and key reuse rules matter. Local deduplication does not provide exactly-once external execution across an uncooperative provider boundary. || Key K and payload hash H / Atomically reserve operation K / Execute or reconcile provider operation / Persist and replay the same result

Authentication establishes the caller's identity under a trust model; authorization decides whether that caller may perform this operation on this resource. Derive tenant context from authenticated membership, not a freely supplied tenant_id alone. GET /payments/42 must check resource ownership even if payment identifiers are hard to guess. Coarse gateway checks complement, rather than replace, domain authorization. Validate service-to-service credentials as well as user credentials when requests cross internal boundaries. || Validate credential / Resolve permitted tenant and roles / Load resource under tenant constraint / Authorize the requested action

A rate limit controls request pace; a quota limits accumulated use over a period or resource dimension. A payment API might limit expensive validation attempts and separately cap daily exports. Define keys, windows, burst behavior, retry advice, and failure policy. One global limit can let a noisy tenant harm everyone, while per-user limits can miss coordinated abuse. Distributed enforcement needs atomic state or an explicit approximation and regional allocation contract. || Identify tenant and operation / Check burst and sustained allowance / Admit or reject with retry guidance / Account for distributed enforcement scope

Retries should fit an overall deadline and target transient, safe-to-repeat work. Budget time for connection acquisition, attempts, and backoff; propagate remaining time downstream. Exponential backoff with jitter reduces synchronized retries but does not create capacity. Avoid retries independently at every layer: three attempts at each of four layers can amplify one intent into 81 attempts. For side effects, retries need stable identity and reconciliation of uncertain outcomes. || Parent deadline established / Attempt consumes remaining time / Backoff includes jitter / Stop when budget or safe retry condition ends

A long-running operation can return an operation resource and let clients poll or subscribe for progress. Streaming returns a sequence with explicit framing, cancellation, buffering, and resume policy. A disconnected client does not necessarily cancel the business job; define who owns it after submission. Persist required job state outside request-local memory. Avoid holding a database transaction open for the lifetime of a slow stream; read bounded pages or transfer a deliberate streaming resource owner. || Submit operation / Persist job and return operation ID / Worker advances durable state / Client observes or cancels under contract

A webhook is an asynchronous request to another party. Sign it, include a stable event identifier and timestamp policy, and accept acknowledgments according to a documented delivery contract. A receiver should commit its inbox or effect before acknowledging if persistence is required. A sender can retry a lost acknowledgment, so consumers must deduplicate. A 2xx response proves only what the receiving contract promises, not that every downstream business task has completed. || Sender signs event E / Receiver verifies and stores E / Receiver acknowledges receipt / Lost acknowledgment causes duplicate delivery

Schema evolution applies to stored data and events as well as synchronous APIs. Preserve meanings, defaults, field identity, and unknown-value behavior. A consumer replaying a year of events may encounter every historical version, so deleting a decoder can break recovery even if current traffic looks healthy. Use explicit versions or upcasters with tested historical samples. An additive field is not harmless if it changes a signature calculation, a uniqueness rule, or a required business decision. || Historical event version 1 / New decoder supports old meaning / Version 2 adds explicit field / Replay validates both histories

Consumer-driven contracts record assumptions that real consumers make about a provider. Run provider verification against those contracts before release, while also testing behavior that schemas miss. A fixture asserting that status is a string does not protect a client that assumes every returned status is final. Contracts can become brittle if they freeze irrelevant formatting. Focus on observable obligations and maintain representative error, authorization, pagination, and retry scenarios alongside success examples. || Consumer states required behavior / Provider verifies contract / Release checks compatible semantics / Runtime telemetry catches unmodeled behavior

A gateway provides shared edge concerns; a Backend for Frontend adapts APIs for a particular client experience. A mobile BFF can aggregate necessary data and reduce chatty requests, but adds latency, capacity, and ownership concerns. Keep resource-level authorization in the systems that understand ownership. Avoid a universal aggregation service that every product must change together. Cache only representations whose key, identity scope, and freshness policy are well defined. || Mobile client requests screen data / BFF aggregates bounded calls / Gateway applies edge policy / Domain services enforce ownership

An internal API still crosses a trust, version, or failure boundary. Internal clients may have different release schedules and can send malformed or excessive requests. A public API generally needs stronger compatibility commitments, discoverability, abuse control, and support processes, but internal status does not justify skipping authentication or invariants. Document audience and lifetime explicitly. A service initially called “private” often becomes a dependency whose accidental behavior is difficult to change. || Identify API audience / Define compatibility duration / Enforce identity and capacity policy / Observe consumers before changing behavior

Documentation should explain complete operation histories: request examples, errors, authorization, pagination, timeouts, idempotency, and asynchronous status. Machine-readable schemas help generate clients, while prose defines semantics those schemas cannot express. A runnable sandbox must distinguish simulated effects from real charges. Track documentation versions with implementation, and test key examples. Discoverability is practical: users should know how to find the right operation, determine its scope, and recover when the happy path fails. || Find operation / Read schema and behavioral contract / Try safe example / Follow documented recovery path''')
